<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ajmer</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ajayameru</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ajmer is a city in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, among the Aravalli hills. Founded as Ajayameru by the Chahamana king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajayaraja-ii">Ajayaraja II</a> in the early 12th century, it was the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prithviraj-chauhan">Prithviraj Chauhan</a> before the Ghurid conquest of 1192. It is famous for the shrine of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> saint Moinuddin Chishti, a major pilgrimage centre; under British rule it formed the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajmer-merwara">Ajmer-Merwara</a> and it joined Rajasthan in 1956.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ajmer.jpg" alt="Ajmer" width="320" height="188" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: SUDHIR UPADHYAY JI · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ADHAI_DIN_KA_JHONPRA.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
